WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.798
Every network message should be parsed defensively.

2
00:00:02.798 --> 00:00:09.592
Length fields, counts, offsets, command names, identifiers, encodings, and nested structures all require validation before allocation, copying,

3
00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:11.191
indexing, or business logic.

4
00:00:11.191 --> 00:00:20.383
A parser should assume that input can be incomplete, inconsistent, or intentionally outside the normal range, and it should reject that input before

5
00:00:20.383 --> 00:00:21.981
it affects application state.

6
00:00:21.981 --> 00:00:23.580
Validation should be specific.

7
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.978
A maximum message size protects memory.

8
00:00:25.978 --> 00:00:28.776
A maximum header count protects parser work.

9
00:00:28.776 --> 00:00:32.373
A maximum nesting depth protects recursive or structured formats.

10
00:00:32.373 --> 00:00:37.968
Type checks, range checks, encoding checks, and version checks make the protocol contract executable.

11
00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:42.764
If a field is optional, the parser should define the default behavior.

12
00:00:42.764 --> 00:00:47.560
If a field is required, absence should fail in a controlled way.

13
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.158
Resource limits protect availability.

14
00:00:49.158 --> 00:00:57.951
A program can be correct for one request and still fail under many slow connections, many queued jobs, or many oversized messages.

15
00:00:57.951 --> 00:01:06.344
Limits on connection count, queued work, request size, parse depth, retained buffers, and per-peer rate can keep resource use predictable.

16
00:01:06.344 --> 00:01:11.939
The limits should be observable so operators can distinguish normal pressure from configuration problems.

17
00:01:11.939 --> 00:01:14.737
Parsing should be separated from business logic.

18
00:01:14.737 --> 00:01:21.131
Raw network bytes should become validated structures before they influence authorization, routing, storage, or state transitions.

19
00:01:21.131 --> 00:01:26.327
Malformed input should release temporary resources and avoid detailed responses that expose internals.

20
00:01:26.327 --> 00:01:34.320
This separation makes code review easier because reviewers can see where trust changes from untrusted input to validated protocol data.
